# ffi/logs/rust/stage3-inlining-term.log
# W3 audit: separating "was not inlined" from "crossed a boundary and materialised a group".
# 2026-09-18
#
# CONFIGURATION (R7)
#   machine        4 vCPU Intel Xeon @ 2.80GHz, 15 GB, Ubuntu 24.04.4, Linux 6.18.44 x86_64,
#                  container, NOT an isolated host. No CPU pinning, no governor control.
#   rust           rustc/cargo 1.94.1, profile release (opt-level 3, lto OFF, codegen-units
#                  16, PIE, default relocation model). No [profile] override in the workspace.
#   MSRV declared  1.88. NOT VERIFIED: no 1.88 toolchain exists in this container.
#   incumbent      prost 0.14.4, carried as the in-process control on every row
#   LINKAGE        cdylib (libak_core.so), dynamic linker. Not a static link.
#   guard          on
#   class          M1 (P1.1 to P1.3) is a REAL message (design/SHAPES.md). No control rows.
#   content        ASCII.
#
# WHAT IS BEING AUDITED, AND BY WHOSE OBJECTION
#   The per-element interface cost quoted for M1 -- 40.5 ns on P1.1, 44.9 on P1.2, 11.1 on
#   P1.3 -- was obtained by subtracting `core-native` from `core-ffi-rust`. The objection:
#   `core-native` is compiled into the harness, so rustc can fuse the traversal into the
#   benchmark loop and keep the writer state in registers, and the FFI arm cannot. That
#   subtraction would then bundle "was not inlined" into "crossed a boundary and
#   materialised a 200-byte group", and the figure would be an UPPER BOUND quoted as if it
#   were the interface term alone.
#
# THE ANSWER, BEFORE THE NUMBERS
#   1. THE PREMISE IS FALSE FOR THESE BINARIES, AND THE ARTIFACT SAYS SO. In the `bench`
#      binary that produced the published figures, the largest `bench::main::{{closure}}`
#      is 472 bytes. The traversal it would have to contain is 4,299 bytes on encode
#      (`enc_list_results_response`) and 11,311 on decode (`decode_list_results_response`).
#      No closure can hold an inlined copy of either. `encode_into_list_results_response` is
#      a 20-byte thunk, and both entry points are exported globals in a PIE, so they are
#      reached through the GOT with `call *0x..(%rip)` -- an INDIRECT call, structurally the
#      same shape as the call into the cdylib. There was no inlining advantage to remove.
#   2. THE TWO NEW ARMS CONFIRM IT BY MEASUREMENT. `core-native-noinline` and
#      `core-native-opaque` measure the same as `core-native` to within the run-to-run
#      spread on every row. The inlining term is at most +-0.6 ns per element on encode.
#   3. SO THE DECOMPOSITION STANDS. On P1.3 encode the inlining term is -0.10 to +0.01 ns
#      per element against a group term of 11.3 to 11.4; on P1.3 decode it is -1.03 to
#      -0.82 against 27.6 to 28.4. The absent-path inversion is not an inlining artifact.
#   4. ONE CORRECTION IS STILL DUE, AND IT IS NOT THE ONE THE OBJECTION NAMED. On P1.1 and
#      P1.2 DECODE the two arms are within each other's spread and the sign of the
#      difference is not stable across builds: it was `core-native` faster in the `bench`
#      binary and `core-ffi-rust` faster here. A per-element interface cost should not be
#      quoted for those two rows in either direction. P1.3 decode is the row with a real
#      effect, and it is the one being audited.
#   5. AND THE CALL IS NOT THE COST. The counting build says 9 crossings for 1000 elements
#      on P1.2 encode and 4 for 300 on P1.3, so at the 1.8 ns already measured the dynamic
#      call contributes about 0.02 ns per element. The `group+call` column is group
#      materialisation with a rounding error attached.
#
# WHY IT IS NOT INLINED: THE MECHANISM, STATED PRECISELY
#   Added after the fact, because the artifact evidence above is the symptom and this is the
#   reason. It is also narrower than "LTO is off, so nothing crosses the crate boundary",
#   which is NOT true of this file.
#
#   - The workspace `Cargo.toml` has NO `[profile.release]` section, so cargo's default
#     applies: opt-level 3, `lto = false`, codegen-units 16.
#   - With LTO off, a function in `facade` is available for inlining into `harness` only if
#     its MIR is exported cross-crate, which means generic or `#[inline]`.
#   - **The two entry points the benchmark calls are neither.**
#     `encode_into_list_results_response` and `decode_list_results_response` are plain
#     non-generic `pub fn` with no `#[inline]`, so their bodies cannot cross into `harness`
#     at all. That is the load-bearing fact.
#   - **It is NOT true that nothing in that file can be inlined across the boundary.**
#     `core_native.rs` carries 38 `#[inline]` functions, including
#     `enc_list_results_response` itself, whose MIR IS exported. It was not inlined because
#     it is 4,299 bytes and no cost model will take that into a 472-byte closure -- and
#     because the benchmark never calls it directly: it calls the 20-byte entry thunk, which
#     is the one that genuinely cannot cross.
#
#   So the premise this log refutes is refuted for a specific and checkable reason, and the
#   reason has a failure mode: **if the generator ever put `#[inline]` on a per-message entry
#   point, or made one generic, the objection could become true again with LTO still off.**
#   `gen/inline_check.sh` would show it -- the closure would grow past the traversal -- which
#   is why that check is a script and not a paragraph.
#
# THE TWO ARMS, AND WHY BOTH
#   core-native            the existing no-boundary control.
#   core-native-noinline   `#[inline(never)]` on the per-message entry point. A real call,
#                          same crate, rustc may still reason about the body. A LOWER BOUND
#                          on any inlining penalty.
#   core-native-opaque     the same entry point reached through a function pointer that has
#                          been through `black_box`: no inlining, no devirtualisation, no
#                          constant propagation across the call. The closer model of a
#                          dynamic call into a cdylib, MINUS the group.
#   core-ffi-rust          the real thing: group materialisation + dynamic call.
#
#   Both are at the granularity the FFI call sits at -- the per-message entry point, not the
#   inner per-element traversal -- and both call the same generated traversal, so a byte
#   difference from `core-native` would be a defect in the wrapper and nothing else. Section
#   1 of each run checks exactly that against the validated manifest.
#
# MEASUREMENT HAZARDS THIS TABLE IS EXPOSED TO (R9)
#   1. P1.1 HAS FOUR ELEMENTS. Its per-element column is a per-MESSAGE cost divided by four
#      and is not comparable with P1.2's. It is printed because the published figure was,
#      not because it means the same thing.
#   2. The inlining term is a difference of two numbers of the same size, so on the decode
#      rows it is comparable to the spread. Three runs are printed; read the range.
#   3. Container, shared vCPUs, no pinning. Spread is reported per row.
#   4. THIS IS ONE COMPILER AND ONE LINK. With `lto = "fat"`, or in a build where the
#      traversal is small enough to inline, the premise this refutes could become true.
#      What is shown is that it is false for the binaries these figures came from.
#
